Keeps Chrome from giving a tab it activates the last tab's background colour on Linux and
Windows, as it already doesn't on the Mac. The core gives each page the colour it shows before
it first paints (core/tab.cc), and the copy beat it, so a tab a page opened showed its opener's
colour, white for most pages, until it painted.

--- a/chrome/browser/ui/browser.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/browser.cc
@@ -786,7 +786,10 @@
   // loaded any contents. As a result, we avoid flashing white when moving to
   // a new tab. (There is also code in RenderFrameHostManager to do something
   // similar for intra-tab navigations.)
-  if (selection.old_contents && selection.new_contents) {
+  // browser_core: the core gives each page the colour it shows before it first paints, as on the
+  // Mac, which this would replace with the last tab's.
+  if (selection.old_contents && selection.new_contents &&
+      !g_browser_core_supplies_windows) {
     // While GetPrimaryMainFrame() is guaranteed to return non-null, GetView()
     // is not, e.g. between WebContents creation and creation of the
     // RenderWidgetHostView.
